From 17285af5fad86e41b97b68490c81a004de77bc1e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Zaugg <jzaugg@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 15:04:56 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Record the permitted subclasses of Java sealed classes in
 the API

Without pipelining, Zinc takes the API of Java classes from `ClassToAPI`,
whose `childrenOfSealedClass` only handled enums. Adding a class to the
`permits` of a Java sealed interface left its API unchanged, so a Scala match
on it that was no longer exhaustive was not recompiled.

Read `Class.getPermittedSubclasses` (reflectively, as it needs JDK 17) and,
when there are any, record them as the children and mark the class sealed,
as for Scala sealed classes.

java-sealed-permits-exhaustivity, from rz#14, comes along as a passing
test; it runs on "2.13.y", since this is not bridge code.

(Port of rz#21 to develop. rz#21 sits on the 1.x bridge stack, but this
commit touches only ClassToAPI.)
