From 2e4b60868112517c21863cf4e2be68e0f95e6bae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Zaugg <jzaugg@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 07:57:43 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Depend on the sealed classes below a match's scrutinee

A Java sealed interface can permit a sealed interface from another source:
`S permits A, T` and `T extends S permits B`. When `C` joins `T`, a Scala
match on `S` with cases for `A` and `B` is no longer exhaustive, but `S.java`
is unchanged, so the API of `S` is not even recomputed, and the client
depends on `S`, `A` and `B`, not on `T`, whose children did change. Even
when they share a source, the children of `S` (`A` and `T`) stay the same.

A Scala sealed hierarchy lives in one source and `ExtractAPI` records all
of `sealedDescendants` as the children of the root, so Scala never needed
this.

For a pattern match, the bridge now also records a member reference to each
sealed class below the sealed classes of the scrutinee's type, and uses
their names in both the default and the pattern-match scopes, so a change
to their children reaches the client however `useOptimizedSealed` is set.
As for the scrutinee itself, the scrutinee type is read from the `Match`,
or, where the bridge phases run after patmat (Scala 2.12), from the
synthetic case `ValDef` that patmat leaves in its place.

`java-sealed-nested-permits` (S2 in rz#42) now passes, and
`java-sealed-nested-permits-same-source` covers the single-source variant
the conformance harness found.
